@startuml 00-bounded-context-map
title Spottrack Platform — Bounded Context Map (index of the per-context class diagrams in this folder)

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false

note as Legend
  Each bounded context below has its own small, focused class-diagram file
  in this folder (e.g. gym-equipment-domain-model.puml). This map only shows
  context-to-context DEPENDENCIES (derived from actual cross-package imports),
  not class detail — splitting the model this way is what keeps any single
  diagram from becoming a "big ball of mud".
end note

package "shared" as shared <<Shared Kernel>> #EFEFEF

package "iam" as iam
package "profiles" as profiles
package "gym" as gym
package "membership" as membership
package "reservation" as reservation
package "maintenance" as maintenance
package "monitoring" as monitoring
package "analytics" as analytics
package "routine" as routine

package "alert (misplaced inside shared.*)" as alert #FFE0E0

alert ..> shared

' every context depends on the shared kernel (omitted per-arrow for readability)
iam ..> shared
profiles ..> shared
gym ..> shared
membership ..> shared
reservation ..> shared
maintenance ..> shared
monitoring ..> shared
analytics ..> shared
routine ..> shared

' real cross-context dependencies, derived from import scans
gym --> iam
gym --> maintenance
gym --> membership
gym --> profiles
gym --> reservation

iam --> profiles

maintenance --> gym
maintenance --> iam

membership --> iam
membership --> profiles

monitoring --> gym
monitoring --> iam
monitoring --> profiles
monitoring --> reservation

profiles --> gym
profiles --> iam

reservation --> gym
reservation --> iam
reservation --> monitoring
reservation --> profiles

analytics --> gym
analytics --> iam
analytics --> monitoring

routine --> iam
routine --> profiles

note bottom of gym
  <b>Cyclic dependencies detected</b> (bounded contexts import
  each other's domain types both ways — no clean one-directional
  dependency, which undermines context isolation):
  • gym <-> profiles
  • gym <-> reservation
  • iam  <-> profiles
  • reservation <-> monitoring
  A DDD-cleaner alternative: replace direct type imports across
  contexts with domain events + anti-corruption layers, or typed
  IDs only (no behavior/entity imports) in the dependent direction.
end note

note bottom of routine
  routine has no incoming or cyclic edges — the most cleanly
  isolated context in the codebase (see routine-domain-model.puml).
end note

note bottom of alert
  Alert has its own aggregate, repository, application services and
  REST controller, but is physically packaged under shared.* alongside
  the real shared kernel (Money, AbstractDomainAggregateRoot, Result).
  Nothing about it is actually shared/reused — this is the one spot in
  the codebase already drifting toward "big ball of mud" packaging.
  See alert-domain-model.puml.
end note
@enduml
